  "title": "Long ants standing still in a crowd",
  "question": "Scrub both strips. Does the pile dissolve -- do the animals on the soil keep rearranging themselves rather than sitting in the same place frame after frame -- and does what movement there is read as ants sorting themselves out rather than teleporting?",
  "context_md": "Your report, 2026-09-11: *\"long ants getting stuck. Not all of them but it happens regularly. It seems like they get stuck in a big group/pile of long ants.\"*\n\nThe same bed, the same seed, the same 3,000 frames (28,000-31,000), 31 stops. The pale bodies on and just above the soil line are the ants.\n\n**What changed.** A carrying long ant that has nowhere to go waits for the nestmate in its way to move instead of turning round -- and it used to wait for ever, because that nestmate is usually stuck for the same reason. One arm still waits for ever. The other gives it four beats and then turns round anyway.\n\n**The cost, so you are not surprised by it:** a colony that stops standing still walks more and eats more, and the one in the changed arm ends up much smaller and hungrier while carrying more food home. The numbers under each strip are from a separate 120,000-frame run on this seed, not from these 3,000 frames.",
